# A message that names us, under the server's casemap, is a highlight
# unless a letter or digit touches an end of the match that is itself a
# letter or digit (Textual's normal matching): "tex[]s" highlights,
# "xtex[]" does not. Our own lines never are. Only what others say counts as unread: not events,
# not our own echoes, never the console. A channel NOTICE neither counts
# nor highlights (Textual only notifies); a NOTICE in an open query counts
# as unread but never highlights.
= nick tex[]
! welcome echo-message
< :tex[]!u@h JOIN #c
> MODE #c
? unread #c 0 highlights 0
< :bob!b@h PRIVMSG #c :hey TEX{}: look
? unread #c 1 highlights 1
< :bob!b@h PRIVMSG #c :tex[]s are cool
< :bob!b@h PRIVMSG #c :xtex[] no
? unread #c 3 highlights 2
< :bob!b@h NOTICE #c :(tex[]) notice
? unread #c 3 highlights 2
< :tex[]!u@h PRIVMSG #c :tex[] talking to myself
< :carol!c@h JOIN #c
? unread #c 3 highlights 2
< :bob!b@h PRIVMSG tex[] :private, tex[]
? unread bob 1 highlights 1
< :bob!b@h NOTICE tex[] :a notice for tex[]
? unread bob 2 highlights 1
< :irc.test NOTICE tex[] :tex[] from the server
? unread console 0 highlights 0
? registered tex[]
